# Summary of changes for run 285a919f-691f-49dd-84ea-96d79e05e3f1
Completed a research cycle on **Rademacher Complexity of Neural Networks** under the MachineLearning domain. Two new Lean files were added under `Catalog/MachineLearning/Rademacher/`, both building cleanly with **0 sorries** and only the standard axioms (`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).

**`Basic.lean` — foundations of empirical Rademacher complexity.**
Defines `empRad n A` as the average over all `2^n` sign patterns of the best in-class correlation (a faithful empirical Rademacher complexity for a finite hypothesis class evaluated on an `n`-point sample) and proves its core structural laws:
- `sum_sgn_coord_eq_zero` — Rademacher signs are mean-zero (discrete `E_σ[σ]=0`), via a coordinate-flip involution;
- `empRad_singleton` — a single hypothesis has zero complexity;
- `empRad_mono` — monotone under class inclusion;
- `empRad_nonneg` — every nonempty class has nonnegative complexity (no symmetry needed);
- `empRad_smul` — positive homogeneity (`empRad (c•A) = c·empRad A` for `c ≥ 0`).

**`NeuralNet.lean` — depth, spectral norm, weight normalization, generalization.**
Models an `L`-layer network as iterated spectrally-bounded linear layers and derives:
- `deepNet_eq` / `empRad_deepNet` — an `L`-layer net with per-layer spectral factor `c` scales the complexity by exactly `c^L`;
- `empRad_deepNet_le_of_normalized` and `empRad_deepNet_antitone_depth` — under weight normalization (`c ≤ 1`) depth never increases, and strictly contracts, the complexity;
- `normBall_subset` / `empRad_weightNorm_mono` — shrinking the spectral-norm budget shrinks the complexity (the precise sense in which weight normalization helps);
- `genGap` / `genGap_mono_rad` — the standard Rademacher uniform-deviation generalization bound `2R + √(log(1/δ)/(2n))` is monotone in the complexity;
- `weightNorm_improves_genGap` and `deepNet_normalized_genGap_le` — combining the above into the chain "normalize weights ⇒ lower Rademacher complexity ⇒ tighter generalization bound."

Each file contains `-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--` blocks documenting the Hypothesize/Experiment/Analyze/Critique/Synthesize loop. Honesty note: the exact geometric `c^L` depth law is proved; the literature's tighter `O(C·√L/√n)` refinement (which needs Talagrand contraction and Massart's finite-class lemma) is not proved here and is recorded as an explicit open direction rather than claimed.

Supporting documents: `ComputationalEvidence.md` (small-case numerics matching each theorem and a counterexample hunt that found none) and `FUTURE_DIRECTIONS.md` (five bold, falsifiable conjectures — Talagrand contraction, the `√L` refinement, strict normalization monotonicity, a PAC-Bayes⇄Rademacher bridge, and a sample-size decay law — each with a "key insight" and "why now").

The new files are automatically part of the `MachineLearning` build target, so they are verified as part of the project.
